15 0 obj
<< /A 53 0 R /Alt (Aerial view and illustrations of solar panel arrays. Panel \(a\) shows extensive solar panels installed in a rural area near a river. Panel \(b\) depicts an overhead view of solar panels arranged in a grid pattern. Panel \(c\) illustrates three configurations: full-density \(FD\), high-density \(HD\), and moderate-density \(MD\) solar panel structures, each on different support frameworks.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 37 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
16 0 obj
<< /A 54 0 R /Alt (Photograph depicting a solar panel structure in an agricultural setting, bordered by evenly spaced vertical beams. Adjacent to it, a diagram illustrates the monitoring setup. It highlights ground measurements for solar radiation, air temperature, humidity, rainfall, wind speed, and direction, alongside underground monitoring for soil temperature and moisture. The setup includes marked measurements for dimensions and monitoring points.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 38 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
17 0 obj
<< /A 55 0 R /Alt (Flowchart illustrating a data processing and prediction system using a Long Short-Term Memory \(LSTM\) attention model. Sensors upload data for preprocessing and feature selection. Preprocessed data is distributed to a model comprising input, hidden, attention, fully connected, and output layers. The model evaluates performance and stores updated data in a cloud server. Feedback is looped back to the system for local storage and execution. Icons and graphs depict data types and processes at each stage.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 39 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
18 0 obj
<< /A 56 0 R /Alt (Diagram illustrating a Long Short-Term Memory \(LSTM\) cell with three main gates: forget, update, and output. Inputs \\\( C_{t-1} \\\), \\\( x_t \\\), and \\\( o_{t-1} \\\) influence cell state \\\( C_t \\\) and output \\\( h_t \\\). Arithmetic operations, activation functions, and gating mechanisms manage information transfer.) /K 1 /P 8 0 R /Pg 39 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
19 0 obj
<< /A 57 0 R /Alt <> /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 40 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
20 0 obj
<< /A 58 0 R /Alt (Diagram of a neural network architecture with four layers: input, hidden LSTM cell, attention, and fully connected, leading to the output. Each layer processes information sequentially, with arrows indicating data flow.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 41 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
21 0 obj
<< /A 59 0 R /Alt (Heatmap showing correlation coefficients between different variables. Ranges from dark orange to teal, representing values 1 to negative 0.95. Significant correlations include Iat and Isr at 0.73, and Isr and Esr at 0.98.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 42 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
22 0 obj
<< /A 60 0 R /Alt (Six line graphs comparing solar radiation intensity over time using different modeling techniques: Measured data, BP, SVM, LSTM, LSTM-GRU, and LSTM-Attention. Graphs are labeled \(a\) and \(b\) for MD, HD, and FD, showing variations in intensity readings for each method over a 600-minute period.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 43 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
23 0 obj
<< /A 61 0 R /Alt (Six bar charts compare evaluation indicators for different models across seasons and conditions: MD, HD, and FD. The models\204SVM, BP, LSTM, LSTM-GRU, and LSTM-Attention\204are evaluated using RSME, MAE, and R-squared. Top row represents summer; bottom row represents winter. Top graphs show variability across conditions; winter graphs have consistent R-squared values.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 44 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
24 0 obj
<< /A 62 0 R /Alt (Graphs showing solar radiation intensity over time on different days.   Top panel: Three graphs \(MD, HD, FD\) for June 2023. Peaks on sunny days, lower values on cloudy and rainy days.   Bottom panel: Three graphs \(MD, HD, FD\) for December 2023. Similar pattern with peaks on sunny days and smaller peaks on cloudy and rainy days.   Colors represent measured data and three prediction models: LSTM, LSTM-GRU, and LSTM-Attention.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 45 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
25 0 obj
<< /A 63 0 R /Alt (Two sets of line graphs compare measured data and various prediction models for air temperature over time. The left side shows graphs with a temperature range of -10 to 45 degrees Celsius. The right side's range is -10 to 25 degrees Celsius. Each set is titled MD, HD, and FD. Models include BP, SVM, LSTM, LSTM-GRU, and LSTM-AT. Time is measured in ten-minute intervals up to 1,280 minutes.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 47 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
26 0 obj
<< /A 64 0 R /Alt (Bar charts comparing six models: SVM, BP, LSTM, LSTM-GRU, and LSTM-Attention. Metrics displayed are RSME, MAE, and R-squared for three scenarios: MD, HD, and FD, presented in two rows. Each chart shows decreasing error and increasing R-squared values across models.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 48 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
27 0 obj
<< /A 65 0 R /Alt (Graph \(a\) and \(b\) depict air temperature data using various models. Each graph contains three panels representing different conditions: sunny, cloudy, and rainy days. The top panel covers MD, HD, and FD from June 15 to June 19, 2023, with temperatures between 15 and 50°C. The bottom panel portrays MD, HD, and FD from December 10 to December 21, 2023, with temperatures ranging from -20 to 30°C. Models include measured data, LSTM, LSTM-GRU, and LSTM-Attention, showing similar temperature trends across conditions.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 49 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
